<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Idumea</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1206 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Livonian-Latgalian district in northern Latvia where the chronicler Henry of Livonia served as a parish priest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Idumea lay north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauja">Gauja</a> river, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Liv</a> lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turaida">Turaida</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/metsepole">Metsepole</a> and the Latgalian land of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/talava">Tālava</a>. Its population was mixed Livonian and Latgalian, and it appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia's</a> chronicle in the early 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its people lived in small farming communities under local elders. After the Germans arrived they accepted baptism, and Henry himself, the author of the chronicle, was ordained and served as priest among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumeans</a> from about 1208.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Idumea came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Riga around 1206 and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a>. Its people were absorbed into the Latvian population.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1100 CE – 1206 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.59%;width:2.11%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1206 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic and Finnic paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Livonian, Latgalian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Idumeja, Ydumea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1153&amp;focus=idumea" aria-label="Idumea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5229 234 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Idumea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5549 490h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5549 490h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Latvia. <a href="/atlas?year=1153&amp;focus=idumea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

